{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>YHWH alone did lead him, and there was with him no strange god.</b> In tractate Berakhot (6a) they expounded the verse ״אֶת ה׳ הֶאֱמַרְתָּ הַיּוֹם״ (Deuteronomy 26:17): you made Me a single entity in the world, as it says ״שְׁמַע יִשְׂרָאֵל ה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ ה׳ אֶחָד״ (ibid. 6:4); ״וַה׳ הֶאֱמִירְךָ״ and so forth (ibid.) — to make you a single entity in the world, as it says (I Chronicles 17:21) ״וּמִי כְּעַמְּךָ יִשְׂרָאֵל גּוֹי אֶחָד״. This is what it says: ״YHWH alone did lead him״ — that is to say, He made Israel a single entity in the world, for He led them apart, as it is written (Numbers 23:9) ״הֶן עָם לְבָדָד יִשְׁכֹּן״. And ״alone״ (בָּדָד) refers to Israel, for He set them apart from the nations to be one people uniquely devoted to Him, blessed be He — they are alone, by themselves, and not the nations. But on this condition: when ״there was with him no strange god״ — with Israel — meaning that they make Him, blessed be He, a single entity in the world; then accordingly this too Elohim did (Ecclesiastes 7:14)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "The Talmudic passage at Berakhot 6a inverts the subject and object of הֶאֱמַרְתָּ and הֶאֱמִירְךָ; the Kli Yakar renders the derash faithfully. The verse citations as printed in the source carry reference numbers that do not always match the chapter divisions of the standard text; they have been normalized to the canonical addresses."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:15:57.426909316Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}